-- title:
--   Proposition 6 — diminishing stepsize, cyclic order: $\liminf F(x_k)=F^*$, and $x_k\to x^*\in X^*$ when $\sum\alpha_k^2<\infty$
-- statement:
--   Consider problem (5)–(6): minimize $F(x)=\sum_{i=1}^mF_i(x)$, $F_i=f_i+h_i$, over a nonempty closed convex set $X\subseteq\mathbb R^n$, with all $f_i,h_i:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ convex. Let $F^*=\inf_{x\in X}F(x)$ and $X^*=\{x^*\in X:F(x^*)=F^*\}$. Let $\{x_k\}$ be a sequence generated by any one of the incremental subgradient-proximal iterations (19), (20), (21) with the cyclic order $i_k=(k\bmod m)+1$, under Assumption 1 (for (19), (20)) or Assumption 2 (for (21)) with constant $c$, and with positive stepsizes $\alpha_k$, constant within each cycle, such that
--   $$\lim_{k\to\infty}\alpha_k=0,\qquad\sum_{k=0}^\infty\alpha_k=\infty.$$
--   Then
--   $$\liminf_{k\to\infty}F(x_k)=F^*.$$
--   Furthermore, if $X^*$ is nonempty and $\sum_{k=0}^\infty\alpha_k^2<\infty$, then $\{x_k\}$ converges to some $x^*\in X^*$.
--
--   This is the main convergence result of the cyclic analysis: with a diminishing, non-summable stepsize the incremental methods reach the optimal value, and with square-summable stepsizes the iterates themselves converge to an optimal solution.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The proposition's sentence does not repeat Assumptions 1–2, but the paper assumes them "throughout this section" (p. 8), so they are explicit hypotheses here, together with the standing assumptions of p. 4 (convexity, $X$ nonempty closed convex), positivity of the stepsizes (p. 2), their constancy within a cycle (p. 7) and $m\ge1$. The starting point $x_0$ is arbitrary, as in the paper. $\liminf F(x_k)$ and $F^*$ are taken in `EReal`, so $F^*=-\infty$ is allowed in the first part and no boundedness is assumed. $\sum\alpha_k=\infty$ is divergence of the partial sums; $\sum\alpha_k^2<\infty$ is `Summable`. Components are 0-based (`Fin m`), so $i_k$ is `k % m`.
-- source:
--   Bertsekas, Incremental Proximal Methods for Large Scale Convex Optimization, LIDS-P-2847 (rev. March 2011), §3.2, p. 14, Proposition 6 (Assumptions 1–2, pp. 8–9)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_IncProx_Cyclic_Basic

namespace IncProx.Cyclic

open Filter Topology

/-- Proposition 6 (p. 14). For a sequence generated by iteration (19), (20) or (21) with the cyclic
order, positive stepsizes constant within each cycle with α_k → 0 and Σ α_k = ∞, under
Assumption 1 or 2 with constant `c`: liminf F(x_k) = F* (in `EReal`). Furthermore, if X* is
nonempty and Σ α_k² < ∞, then x_k converges to some x* ∈ X*. -/
theorem proposition6 {n m : ℕ} [NeZero m] (P : Problem n m) (hP : P.Standing) (A : Alg)
    (α : ℕ → ℝ) (hα : CyclicStepsize m α) (x z gF gH : ℕ → Vec n)
    (hrun : IsRun P A cyc α x z gF gH) (c : ℝ) (hA : AssumptionFor P A x z gF gH c)
    (hα0 : Tendsto α atTop (𝓝 0))
    (hαsum : Tendsto (fun N => ∑ k ∈ Finset.range N, α k) atTop atTop) :
    liminf (fun k => ((P.F (x k) : ℝ) : EReal)) atTop = P.Fstar ∧
    (P.Xstar.Nonempty → Summable (fun k => α k ^ 2) →
      ∃ xs ∈ P.Xstar, Tendsto x atTop (𝓝 xs)) := by sorry

end IncProx.Cyclic
